WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.980
Film Courage: You had early success in your
screenwriting career as you had your first

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:06.350
screenplay optioned for a few thousand dollars
in the beginning?

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.350
Danny Strong: Yes.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.100
Film Courage: And then you wrote for six years
and you were able to sell RECOUNT to HBO.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.300
So most people would have given up in that
six year period.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.420
Especially now when everybody wants things
instantly.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.060
Why didn’t you give up?

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:19.949
Danny Strong: That’s a great question.

00:00:19.949 --> 00:00:23.869
I mean the early success wasn’t really success
[Laughs]…I would say.

00:00:23.869 --> 00:00:26.369
I wouldn’t really call it early success.

00:00:26.369 --> 00:00:29.101
I don’t even view it that way.

00:00:29.101 --> 00:00:35.030
I view it as six years of no one wanting my
stuff and it was some random person who was

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:37.690
very kind who was interested in the script.

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:41.989
But it wasn’t as if it was all of the sudden
“I made it and it’s happening now!”

00:00:41.989 --> 00:00:50.609
So it really was a six, seven year journey
into selling my first project which was recount

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:56.429
and why I didn’t give up I think is because
I had something to say and I loved writing

00:00:56.429 --> 00:01:03.050
these scripts and they were important to me
and I loved working on stories and it just

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:11.090
was artistically, extremely fulfilling where
my acting career was very frustrating.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.710
And I worked…I worked on some terrific shows
that I’m really proud of, but I wasn’t

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:18.799
actually getting to act very much.

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:24.250
So even when you’re working…you know I
would do episodes of Gilmore Girls and I would

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:29.079
do four episodes a season and I would work
on it a day or two and that would be one of

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:31.370
my only jobs for six months.

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:36.010
So it’s really about seven days on set over
a six month period.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.009
So what am I doing the rest of the time?

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:42.930
I’m auditioning and trying to get new jobs
but that’s an hour a day maybe?

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:48.790
So really it was a creative outlet for me
of taking my creative energy and putting it

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:51.420
into something where I can actually just go
do it.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.360
Where as an actor, you have to be picked to
go do it and then you rarely get picked.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:03.490
So I just found it fulfilling but I also was
getting rejected left and right, script after

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.729
script and agents, managers, production companies,
everyone didn’t want my work for six, seven

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:09.819
years.

00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:15.920
And I got to a point where I asked myself
this is not going well.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.610
What do you want from this?

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.360
What are you doing?

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.830
And I sort of asked myself would I (and it’s
one of the key questions in the movie REBEL

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:29.650
IN THE RYE) would I do this for the rest of
my life if I got nothing in return and I thought

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:30.819
“Yeah…I would.”

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:35.160
And I just kept writing and then eventually,
it wasn’t as if six months later…crack

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.250
the champagne!

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:42.870
It was maybe three years later that I sold
RECOUNT as a pitch to HBO and then just kind

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:43.900
of went on from there.

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:53.140
But there was a certain peace in knowing that
it wasn’t about selling something or success

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.860
(even though I really wanted it.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:03:01.770
I wasn’t a Kumbaya moment but there was
a peace in knowing that well whatever happens

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.409
you just keep writing.

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:06.290
So I just kept writing and that’s what I
did.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:09.909
Film Courage: You included that in two parts
of the movie [REBEL IN THE RYE] that premise

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:14.180
[keep writing even if you get nothing in return]
and that’s a real eye-opener that most people

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.250
may not be ready for.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.250
Danny Strong: Yeah.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.129
And look what J.D. Salinger did right?

00:03:18.129 --> 00:03:25.849
He stopped publishing at the height of his
fame and then continued writing for 45 years

00:03:25.849 --> 00:03:30.580
for nothing in return and he wrote every day
and he just did it because he just wanted

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:37.150
to write and I think to me that’s what makes
this story a profound story is the journey

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:42.540
of the artist and he goes from being a young
many who all he wants is success to being

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:48.860
an older writer which all he wants is to just
create his art and nothing else.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.500
And that’s the journey.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.980
That’s the Aesop fable of this movie.

